{"name":"Millennium Silver","hex":"#8c9595","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"millennium","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A period of time consisting of one thousand years.","The year in which one period of one thousand years ends and another begins, especially the year 2000."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The period of one thousand years during which Christ will reign on earth (according to Millenarianist interpretations).","A particular long (and possibly endless) era of universal happiness, peace, and prosperity, predicted by various people to come about someday."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/millennium"},{"term":"silver","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A lustrous, white, metallic element, atomic number 47, atomic weight 107.87, symbol Ag.","Coins made from silver or any similar white metal."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made from silver.","Made from another white metal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silver"}],"entries":[{"title":"Millennium","blocks":[{"text":"A millennium is a period of one thousand years."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Millennium","wikidata":"Q36507"},{"title":"Silver","blocks":[{"text":"Silver is a chemical element; it has symbol Ag and atomic number 47. A soft, white, lustrous transition metal, it exhibits the highest electrical conductivity, thermal conductivity and reflectivity of any metal. Silver is found in the Earth's crust in the pure, free elemental form, as an alloy with gold (electrum) and other metals, and in minerals such as acanthite, argentite and chlorargyrite. Most silver is recovered as a byproduct of lead–zinc, copper and gold mining and refining."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_crystal.jpg/330px-Silver_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver","wikidata":"Q1090"},{"title":"Silv